e country so  that schools can open yeah I was gonna  ask you is it realistic to expect that  schools will make those changes by next  month and and what do you make of  teachers and some teachers unions saying  look we're skeptical we don't feel it's  particularly safe for us as adults to go  back in the classroom well I think if  you're a school you have to identify who  are the high risk teachers those are  people who are elderly have medical  conditions  or who live with them and they need to  be treated slightly different than the  others who don't but you can develop a  plan for teachers who are comfortable  you're not going to force a teacher to  an uncomfortable situation but you have  to develop a plan so kids can go to  school they've done it in many countries  that's been successful children have  more maths in school all day in Japan in  South Korea in China and it's Vietnam  and they've been successful we have to  be able to do it here and we will have  very little spread at the school with a  plan where there are cases then there  will be shutdowns or ways to deal with  those cases doctor I guess the president  said yesterday we will have a vaccine in  therapeutics long before the end of the  year do you agree with that assessment  what's the timeline and more importantly  will it be effective the flu vaccine we  still need to get one every year and  it's not a catch-all glad you're asking  a lot of things at once and what I will  tell you is that the data looking  couraging now that some of the lead  vaccine candidates are causing very  potent immune responses which in general  correlate to some protection from the  virus and I emphasize the word some it's  likely that you will need two shots of  this vaccine to start so one the  beginning of one three weeks later and  then we'll see how long the vaccine  lasts you may need a booster shot every  six months every year we just don't know  yet because we're not that far into the  clinical trials but I am